{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst","id":1493721,"title":"Bloodlines - the best and the worst","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-09-26T07:45:39-07:00","id":1493721,"title":"Bloodlines - the best and the worst","content":"I was thinking of ways to incorporate the bloodlines a bit more into a homebrew V:tM version and I noticed that a lot of the bloodlines leave a lot to be desired. Many of the bloodlines are just the standard Clan but living in some particular place (Anda, Noiad, most of the African versions of the Clans) and some don't have much substance behind them.<br /> <br /> Personally I like the Baali, they are a cool bloodline with a lot of character behind them as well as history. Their role in the setting is almost as strong as that of a full fledged Clan already, even if their numbers are low. I think that the Samedi are also interesting in combining elements of the Nosferatu and Cappadocians with their own unique flavor. I actually like the Daughters of Cacophony as well, even though they are one of the (way too numerous) grrl power bloodlines and based too heavily on their signature Discipline - in part because I have my own background fluff for their (Ventrue) origin.<br /> <br /> What are some of your favorite bloodlines and why do you like them? What makes them interesting and worthy of inclusion in a game or interesting PCs? What are the worst bloodlines that you would never include?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-26T08:15:58-07:00","id":1493728,"title":"","content":"My favorite Bloodline, hands down, is the pre-V5 Nagaraja. I had an amazing game playing as one and I'll never forget it. The balance of trying to secure flesh to eat, while tiding the line of the Masquerade was awesome. The Vitreous Path of Necromancy was also awesome to use, as Wraith is one of my favorite lines in WoD. Interacting with Wraiths in VTM is what I consider to be a high boon. <br /> <br /> Naglopers are another bloodline I really like. Basically the Tzimisce, but African and with a less brutal Clan weakness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-26T10:14:32-07:00","id":1493742,"title":"","content":"My personal favorite is the Kiasyd<br /> I found that &quot;You read for 200 years. You go back to sleep.&quot; is darn silly<br /> First, you have to gather that reading material<br /> My Kiasyds are usually like Indiana Jones, Lara Croft or the the Librarian from the movie franchise: The Librarian; working on the field, collecting books, artifacts, forbidden knowledge! Ancient ruins of an Elder's heaven, a Tremere werehouse, Settite Temple, treasures from the Shadow Court or from the local Necropolis? Tons of adventure to drag my unfortunate Pack/Coterie along... or doing them favors so they have to help me out!<br /> It is not so easy to blend in when you are a tall Elf with picky feeding habits (crystal glasses, fine blood types and so on)<br /> Dancing on the edge of insanity that would put a Malkavian to shame...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-26T12:41:47-07:00","id":1493757,"title":"","content":"Bloodlines are a weird topic because they were created later in the games design life cycle, which means that they are less of main clan but different, and more a refinement of ideas that were used earlier, though not always implemented particularly well. Kind of like how pre V5 Obfuscate is the sneaky discipline, yet Quietus was the one that had the ability to muffle sound, but then became all about blood poison.<br /> <br /> Personally, I have always enjoyed the City Gangrel since the discipline shake up provides such a different feel to them. While it is true that they lack a unique mechanic or their own clan weakness, the playstyle and build approach is more enjoyable for me. <br /> <br /> The bloodline that never made sense to me was the Blood Brothers, they never felt like a fully realized concept. They could have been quite powerful in the Dark Ages setting, but they were designed in the modern nights. They are supposed to be a weaponized form of vampire for use in combat, but between a coterie of Blood Brothers and a coterie of loyalist Gargoyles, the Blood Brothers don't really do much. This is made more apparent when you play the game with canon vampiric population numbers, rather than the more cinematic oversized populations common in many gaming groups, there just aren't enough vampires in any given place to account for a large group of Blood Brothers.<br /> <br /> I have a love hate relationship with the Kiasyd. They felt like the designers took a few different designs, put them through a paper shredder and randomly stuck pieces back together until they had a full idea. I can get behind the idea of the Kiasyd being to Changelings what the Gangrel are to Were-critters. The problems begin with the rest of the design, everything about them screams NPC character. They are lore researchers who like to set up shop in places of education or research and rarely leave. The perfect description of the NPC the PCs track down to gain information or hires the PCs for a job. How so many players over the years have turned them into Indiana Jones or Laura Croft when the write up says they stays home and reads most of the time is beyond me. The Tremere and Giovanni are far better suited to such roles but they never seem to be used as such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-26T18:48:54-07:00","id":1493796,"title":"","content":"The Harbringers of Skulls are my favorite, especially in light of the reveal about them in 5th edition. Alternatively I'm also a big fan of the Witches of Echidna (Setite bloodline).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-26T19:12:43-07:00","id":1493798,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I like the Nagaraja and the Kiasyd as well. I should have mentioned them above. <br /> <br /> I know it\u2019s silly, but the name of the Nagaraja bloodline kinda bugs me. It literally means Snake King and yet they have no snake powers or elements at all. It would have been better to switch their name with the Indian Setites, Daitya, and have the Indian Setites called the Nagaraja. Then again, they aren\u2019t the only WoD faction with a weird name. <br /> <br /> For the Kiasyd, I see them and more than just scholars who never leave the library, so I guess I diverged from the canon a bit, but I find their fluff and imagery really fascinating too.<br /> <br /> I also agree that the Blood Brothers don\u2019t make much sense. Not when the Sabbat already utilizes shovel heads and they Tzimisce have their combat ghouls. I suppose I could see the Blood Brothers arising accidentally from mass embraces with Tzimisce blood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T01:03:11-07:00","id":1493808,"title":"","content":"The Nagaraja have losing the plot built into their clan history, and I love them for it. Charged with protecting Enoch from the Setites by the mummy Inauhaten, made undead by experiments on stolen Setite vitae (hence the clan name, I think)... then the rest of their history is about getting wrapped up in every other damn intrigue in the world, and fighting the Setites ends up not being what they're known for *at all.*<br /> <br /> While I get the appeal of Gargoyles - it was a good cartoon - the bloodline really clearly does date back to a time before every person in the world had a cell phone with a camera. They'd never be introduced if the World of Darkness was being created for the first time today, and there's no way that they fail to take the blame for the Second Inquisition now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T04:56:05-07:00","id":1493817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Reasor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493808#post1493808\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I get the appeal of Gargoyles - it was a good cartoon - the bloodline really clearly does date back to a time before every person in the world had a cell phone with a camera. They'd never be introduced if the World of Darkness was being created for the first time today, and there's no way that they fail to take the blame for the Second Inquisition now.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oddly enough I don't see the cell phone camera being the biggest issue, though it is a huge issue on its own. The proliferation of government backed surveillance technology in most major cities makes for quite a few masquerade problems. In some cities it is to the point that vampires would need maps for dead zones where there isn't any camera coverage.<br /> <br /> Then the fact that you can now purchase infrared sensors that attach to your smart phone means you can basically scan crowds very easily for the ambient temperature bodies walking around.<br /> <br /> The masquerade more or less doesn't function past 2005 in most advanced nations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T10:19:07-07:00","id":1493837,"title":"","content":"Baali: My problem here is that my headcanon is infinitely better than everything the writers seem to want to do with these guys.<br /> I love the blood brothers. I wish they could embrace or have people join their circle.<br /> I love the Samedi but I think the Thanatosis situation is messy. Necromancy would make more sense for them. Thanatosis might work if we changed how Protean functioned.<br /> Gargoyles are great. But Visceraticka should be a collection of alt protean powers (like Thanatosis)<br /> Liahman... I see Ogham as Koldunic sorcery. Works better like that.<br /> Caucophany: All female bloodlines that last for more than a century shouldn't really be a thing. Sex influences mortals but if you put any thought into it there's many reasons why vampires would be less discriminate.<br /> <br /> Kiasyd aren't mechanically sound. Like, it would just be too hard to be a neonate Kiasyd. <b>Maegar</b> are much better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493796#post1493796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Harbringers of Skulls are my favorite, especially in light of the reveal about them in 5th edition. Alternatively I'm also a big fan of the Witches of Echidna (Setite bloodline).</div> </div> </div> </div>Duat have no fury like the settite so scorned: I hate the Witches of Echidna with a passion.<br /> 1: Priests in ancient egypt worked magic. They don't need a magic bloodline to help them out. Furthermore priestly magic (akhu) is very much dissimilar to witchcraft in the way it's practiced, thought of, and so on. <br /> 2: Though the main bloodline are called the priests, that's not to the exclusion of priestesses. Egyptians had priestesses. There's no need for a girl power/fight the patriarchy bloodline. The followers of set aren't a bunch of conservative men worshipping The Man, they're men and women worshipping an anti-establishment figure. Throwing in the mandatory-adversarial-lilith-counterpart when Set already has actually-opposite characters like Amun-Ra or Horus really helps to confuse Set. It's like Set is Red and Lilith is Pink and they're opposites on the colour spectrum... but wait pink and red are pretty close so surely Set must be blue or green... Essentially, we're making Set lesser by pulling this bullshit. Why is 21st century radical feminism attacking relatively progressive egyptians?<br /> 3: Egyptians were writing before Greeks. While Settites do adapt to everything I'd much prefer it if their core beliefs were explained through one mythology.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, they're problematic, because they're written to undermine a faction I like, for reasons that aren't actually founded. They were born because someone who didn't understand Settites decided they didn't like Settites and wanted to knock the Settites down a peg. They're toxic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T10:56:37-07:00","id":1493841,"title":"","content":"All of the girl powers bloodlines, and there are oodles of them, are pretty bad and really don't make any sense.<br /> <br /> Years ago I came up with an alternative origin and explanation for the Daughters of Cacophony, which I like and makes me like them more. The others are bad, especially since there are so many of them. The Ahrimanes are particularly bad in my mind, for redundancy (you don't need them with Gangrel), don't make logical sense, and have a Persian name that has nothing to do with their theme.<br /> <br /> Samedi should have Necromancy and they do in any game I run.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T12:25:06-07:00","id":1493846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sergeant Brother</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493798#post1493798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I know it\u2019s silly, but the name of the Nagaraja bloodline kinda bugs me. It literally means Snake King and yet they have no snake powers or elements at all.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Steven Brown and Andrew Greenberg were, as a pair, really bad about coming up with names that were just completely wrong. Hense the ghoul families bearing the name of vengeful spirits risen from the grave, a 19th century Appalachian offshoot of the Gangrel bearing the name of the ancient Persian demon-god of darkness and lies, and a bunch of cannibal necromancers calling themselves Serpent Kings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T13:24:13-07:00","id":1493849,"title":"","content":"I've always preffered sub clan Bloodlines like warrior setites or Dominate malkavians or destroyed clans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T14:54:18-07:00","id":1493860,"title":"","content":"My opinion is that bloodlines should only exist as a mystery to be explained in the chronicle. If the bloodline does not have an interesting origin, purpose, or other unique facet that adds to the setting, there is no reason to have them. And if an ST introduces them in a specific chronicle, there should be a reason this blood in the game. Otherwise it's just a distrction/fan service.<br /> <br /> So I don't have any bloodlines that are simply members of the same clan by a different name or perhaps a different set of in-clan Disciplines. I hate sub-clans. No Assamite Viziers, Lamia, Anda, Danava, etc. Mechanically they should all be the same as their parent clan. Saying they are a different clan because its members are in an area with a different human culture is just silly. Many of these bloodline names are just alternative names for the clan as a whole. <br /> <br /> Now that most of the secrets behind the Giovanni clan are revealed, I just make the Samedi as Cappadocians whose specific lineage has a Flaw that makes them Monstrous/Cursed. I handle most sub-clans like this if their clan weakness is sufficiently different that it is part of the lore of the game. I give certain lineages of vampires a common Merit or Flaw (usually supernatural in origin) that is passed down through the line. I do it to make these lineages more distinctive. Not all lineages have that, but some do. So it was an easy switch for me to do this and transform certain bloodlines into their main clan.<br /> <br /> So what about the others? Here is what I do in my own chronicles.<br /> <br /> <b>Bloodlines that are actually just cults or sects with secret powers</b><br /> <br /> Baali - I prefer the idea that the Baali are just a cult that has access to a unique Discipline, much like how the Children of Osiris is a cult/sect that has access to a unique Discipline. Neither are bloodlines. Members remain members of their old clans, even if the Baali cult culture denies that is so. I am not that enamored with them as a concept, but they are perfectly fine as a unique example of a long standing infernalist cult. They have their original clan weaknesses, but also gain an additional Flaw to mirror the &quot;bloodline&quot; weakness as well as a result of whatever pact they made.<br /> <br /> Ahrimanes - Like the Baali, not actually a bloodline. They are a self-selecting group of Gangrel who agreed to a participate in a magical ritual that will screw up their vitae in exchange for being able to learn the Spiritus Discipline. The ritual only works on Gangrel vitae because of the unique thaumaturgical experiment Murcia was doing with her own vitae. Interesting only as a faction within a large, chaotic Sabbat for whatever purpose their founder Murcia has for them. The Sabbat merely tolerates them because the sect is about &quot;freedom.&quot; But they do not associate with their sect members for the most part. The Ahrimanes society is considered to be part of the Loyalist camp, and they do cooperate with other Sabbat to keep the centralizers from taking over. They are more like a flavor text to show the diversity within the Sabbat as a very loose camp (not the highly centralized war machine of the &quot;Sword of Caine&quot; of Revised). I'd be totally fine if someone wanted to change their name from Ahrimanes to Amazons or whatever to better reflect their actual traditions.<br /> <br /> <b>Not Truly Vampires</b><br /> <br /> Gargoyles - I've never liked them as actual vampires. They exist in my game, but not as vampires (though it did require the sacrifice of other vampires to create them). They are just an artifact of Tremere power in the Middle Ages. And no new ones have been created in centuries. There are conflicting reports that either the ritual knowledge has been lost, or that the ritual just stopped working. They do need to drink blood to survive since their &quot;energy&quot; needs to come from somewhere. So they are blood drinkers, but not vampires in the sense they are Kindred/Cainites. Meaning identical mechanics for play of game, but cannot actually embrace or count as real vampires.<br /> <br /> Blood Brothers - Similar to the Gargoyles except with the Sabbat. A partially successful attempt by House Goratrix (and some Tzimisce) to provide them servants like the Gargoyles of old. Supernatural blood drinkers with a bad sun allergy, but again not actual vampires in the sense of them being Kindred/Cainites. Each new batch has to be created on its own. Unknown to their creators, Blood Brothers cannot exist forever and will eventually just self-destruct after 50 years or so.<br /> <br /> <b>Actual Bloodlines</b><br /> <br /> \u200bNagaraja - Perfectly fine as a unique bloodline for the True Black Hand revealing its origins as a death cult of Mages before vampires took it over. If you are not using the True Black Hand in some manner related to its original depiction, why have them in your game?<br /> <br /> Lhiannan - In my chronicles, the Lhiannan were the result of the Gangrel Methusaleh the All High attempting to pass on the curse/power to an Unseelie fairy sorceress. This is the origin of the myths that tie Odin (and the Aesir) and Freya (and the Vanir) together although this was done long before the Indo-European peoples ever inhabited Europe. This was not a normal embrace, as it was more of a magical experiment that went horribly wrong. While &quot;the Crone&quot; was the bizarre result of this fairy &quot;turned&quot; vampire, her childer (the Lhiannan vampires) were all once humans because real fairies cannot be turned into vampires. Ogham is a power usable only by them. They are intended to be used as antagonists for Werewolf in elder day before they were finally wiped out. I would never use them in modern Vampire, but might make use of them in a Dark Ages game. My backstory for them is mainly intended to build up the All High as a significant foe for the Garou.<br /> <br /> True Brujah - Pretty much as described but without Temporis Discipline which I find ridiculous. But certain Temporis powers might just be a variant of elder Disciplines for Celerity known only to them. The &quot;False Brujah&quot; are just the Brujah, but their Clan Weakness is a result of the Brujah Antedeluvian cursing Troile and her descendants and twisting, not replacing, Cain's original curse on him and his line. While mostly used as a way to distinguish the True Black Hand's credentials in the Jyhad, their main purpose in my chronicle is to be a bogey man to the Brujah clan (which most Brujah laugh about and do not take seriously). I would probably only ever use NPC True Brujah if I was running a Jyhad level chronicle that would put PCs into contacts with the Inconnu or True Black Hand who would have them as members. Otherwise it'd just be myth and rumors.<br /> <br /> City Gangrel - I find myself compelled to include them only because they are a major part of the Sabbat, and their feud with the &quot;Country Gangrel&quot; is an important part of that sect's politics as originally written. History is the same in the sense that one of the eldest Gangrel who entered the Sabbat is founder of the line, and he quarrelled with the other Gangrel elder who joined. The City Gangrel themselves did not exist before this person fled to the New World. How exactly he changed one of the in-clan Disciplines from Animalism and Fortitude to Celerity and Obfuscate isn't known. I haven't even figured it out myself, but it is likely the result of some forbidden magic, maybe an Infernalist Pact that is now much regretted. There was a big political struggle between these two Gangrel elders in the Sabbat in trying to lead/influence the clan - perhaps involving their opinion on the Gangrel antedeluvian and what being a Gangrel antitribu should mean.<br /> <br /> <b>Nope, these Don't Exist</b><br /> <br /> Kiasyd - I don't think they add anything to the game as their origins are too convoluted. It's possible this was meant as a backdoor way to prepare crossover with the Fairy (later Changeling) game into Vampire. Though I find it funny that since Revised people have mistakenly placed them in the Sabbat when their original description made it very clear they were not Sabbat.<br /> <br /> Laibon - Note sure why these were invented. If you want solitary wandering Kindred from Africa, just use the Gangrel.<br /> <br /> Mariner Gangrel - No. Fish Gangrel do not exist. I find it silly.<br /> <br /> Old Clan Tzimisce - Don't exist because I don't have Vicissitude as a Discipline in my chronicles. All Tzimisce have the Old Clan Disciplines. The &quot;Old Clan Tzimisce&quot; do exist as Tzimisce left overs that survived being destroyed by the Sabbat, but want nothing to do with the Camarilla. But it is a political faction, not a bloodline.<br /> <br /> <b>To Be Determined</b><br /> <br /> Daughters of Cacophony - I really wish I knew what was the original idea behind them. There is something about them that I want to use, but I can't figure out what is the big secret reveal behind them. I imagine it had something to do with Gehenna. <br /> <br /> In my chronicles, I have been playing with the idea that certain vampires not actually be descended from an Antedeluvian. The founders of these unrelated Kindred are a result of Satanic pacts that recreate the curse - but such people are mechanically the same as 7th generation vampires. I intended this as a back door means to explain certain Bloodlines. But I just realized I might not actually need this to explain any of the bloodlines. Who knows, maybe I might use it for the Daughters of Cacophony.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T14:56:33-07:00","id":1493861,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493849#post1493849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've always preffered sub clan Bloodlines like warrior setites or Dominate malkavians or destroyed clans.</div> </div> </div> </div>Likewise. Though I ended up starting to refer to them as Strains to avoid confusion between small b in clan bloodlines and big B separate Bloodlines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T16:08:57-07:00","id":1493870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493837#post1493837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Duat have no fury like the settite so scorned: I hate the Witches of Echidna with a passion.<br /> 1: Priests in ancient egypt worked magic. They don't need a magic bloodline to help them out. Furthermore priestly magic (akhu) is very much dissimilar to witchcraft in the way it's practiced, thought of, and so on.<br /> 2: Though the main bloodline are called the priests, that's not to the exclusion of priestesses. Egyptians had priestesses. There's no need for a girl power/fight the patriarchy bloodline. The followers of set aren't a bunch of conservative men worshipping The Man, they're men and women worshipping an anti-establishment figure. Throwing in the mandatory-adversarial-lilith-counterpart when Set already has actually-opposite characters like Amun-Ra or Horus really helps to confuse Set. It's like Set is Red and Lilith is Pink and they're opposites on the colour spectrum... but wait pink and red are pretty close so surely Set must be blue or green... Essentially, we're making Set lesser by pulling this bullshit. Why is 21st century radical feminism attacking relatively progressive egyptians?<br /> 3: Egyptians were writing before Greeks. While Settites do adapt to everything I'd much prefer it if their core beliefs were explained through one mythology.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, they're problematic, because they're written to undermine a faction I like, for reasons that aren't actually founded. They were born because someone who didn't understand Settites decided they didn't like Settites and wanted to knock the Settites down a peg. They're toxic.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Witches of Echidna were created by the authors to highlight the Typhon/Set conflict within the main clan and, indeed, within Set himself (Echidna being Typhon's wife in Greek mythology). That's also why they are Greek rather than calling themselves Witches of Nephthys or anything else. The idea of Typhon and Greek mythology is pretty important within the concept of VtM Set. They were also intended to help set the stage for what we see in V5 with the Setite civil war, which is one of the V5 developments that I think is pretty cool. Though yeah, if you don't like any of those things then it's natural to dislike the bloodline. <br /> <br /> Also note that while they're called &quot;Witches&quot; the books make it clear that there are many male members as well as female members, so I don't know that they're supposed to be Girl Power or anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T17:53:37-07:00","id":1493876,"title":"","content":"Honestly? Most of the ones I like the most are non-canon ones on STV. For the most part they seem to have had a lot more thought put in than some of the official ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T18:28:03-07:00","id":1493877,"title":"","content":"Ones I use in my games (Or used, rather, it's been a few years since I've run a Vampire game, but this was what I settled on for a slightly alternate head canon, one involving blood potency instead of generation, no second generation and no one really knowing what came before the Clan founders, and everyone being part of the Camarilla except the hardcore cultists in the Sabbat. And I may have forgotten one or two.)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Strains</u></b> (ie, offshoots within the Clans)<br /> <br /> <b>Brujah</b> - None known, though there is the perpetual rumor of a group who've managed to harness and internalize their rage into an icy cold lack of emotion. If they did exist, it'd be more of an alteration to the clan weakness than anything else. <br /> <br /> <b>Gangrel</b> - Maybe the kings of strains. One of the biggest variations is how both Protean and the clan weakness tends to adapt itself the local region and its wildlife, especially regarding the animal forms.<ul><li><i>Shaman</i> - Possessing an affinity for Auspex instead of Fortitude; Part of their weakness includes taking on behavioral taboos.</li> <li><i>Urban</i> - An uncommon variation which first appeared in some of the larger ancient and medieval cities. They have Celerity or Obfuscate in place of Fortitude (we could never decide which fit best), and their weakness focuses on urban animals such as alley cats, rodents, and the like.</li> <li><i>Mariners</i> - Those tied to the seas. Their Protean has minor variations, and their weakness tends to result in things that bear a resemblance to Deep Ones, shark men, kappa, and darker versions of the mermaid legend.</li> </ul><b>Malkavians</b> - They still have the classic Auspex, Dominate, and Obfuscate discipline affinities, with the Dementation powers being alloyed devotions of the three above. There aren't many Strains, so to speak, aside from certain &quot;family&quot; lines who share similar Derangements. <br /> <br /> <b>Nosferatu</b> - Many of their variations are based around common mutations, deformities, and living conditions, but they don't usually have any changes to disciplines or weakness. One example is the <i>Jotun, </i>a lineage mainly found in Scandinavia and neighboring regions who all tend to have the Huge Size merit offset with things like blunt teach, misshapen limbs, and other flaws. <br /> <br /> <b>Toreador</b> - None that anyone is aware of.<br /> <br /> <b>Tremere</b> - Because of their origins, the clan has never really encouraged the development of Strains. Much of their variations tend to come in internal factions who focus on specific areas of Thaumaturgy. But there is one exception.<ul><li><i>Wardens</i> - A holdover from the Dark Ages and the early clan's need for soldiers and guardians, they have an affinity for Fortitude in place of Dominate. Their study of Thaumaturgy tends to focus on warding or combat effective paths.</li> </ul><b>Ventrue</b> - None known, a fact those Ventrue who study such things tend to take pride in. The closest they have to Strains are family lineages who tend to share similar feeding needs.<br /> <br /> <b>Assamites</b> - Unusual in that they are divided almost equally into three groups usually referred to as Castes. All three have an affinity for Auspex, Celerity, and Quietus.<ul><li><i>Philosophers</i> - The main face of the clan has most Western Kindred have known them since the post-Roman period. Scholars, politicos, artists, scientists, engineers, and other people of letters. Their weakness is a fixation on their scholarly areas of interests.</li> <li><i>Judges</i> - Considered the more martially oriented side of the clan, they focus on law, judgment, faith, honor, and responsibility. Their weakness is in their incredibly stubbornness and obsession with rules.</li> <li><i>Magi</i> - The facet of the clan most concerned with the mystical, metaphysical, and arcane, made up of shaman, exorcists, occultists, demon hunters, and other oddities. Their weakness is a focus on occult matters often to the point of distraction from every day affairs. They can, optionally, swap out their affinity for for one of the clan disciplines for that of Assamite Sorcery.</li> </ul>[Yes, this is a clan focused on being truth seekers rather than assassins, and who are a part of regular Kindred society even in Europe and the West rather than some sort of &quot;exotic other.&quot;]<br /> <br /> <b>Giovanni</b> - No Strains. The only real variation is the various internal families and their preferred focuses, but all of them have the standard clan discipline affinities and weakness. <br /> <br /> <b>Ravnos</b> - While there are a number of Jati (or Households as some younger Western Kindred call them) around the globe, there is only one major Strain.<ul><li><i>Seers</i> - who replace Fortitude with Auspex, and are prone to unwanted visions.</li> </ul><b>Setites</b> - While a rather diverse number of factions exist within the clan, these are all based around interpretations of the clan's origins, what sort of myth-lore they draw inspiration from, and how they implement those beliefs. None of them deviate from the clan's discipline affinities or weakness, though some are able to develop an affinity for Setite blood magic when their blood becomes potent enough. There is a single exception.<ul><li><i>Warriors</i> - With an affinity for Potence in place of Obfuscate. Masters or war, they include everything from revolutionaries and holy warriors to chaotic terrorists and cult leaders.</li> </ul><b>Lasombra</b> - None known.<br /> <br /> <b>Tzimisce</b> - Rivaling the Gangrel for their diversity.<ul><li><i>The Families </i>or <i>the Brood</i> - Those embraced from the clan's carefully cultivated thrall families, retaining that family's discipline affinities and weakness in addition to the clan's weakness.</li> <li><i>The Koldun</i> or <i>the Strigori </i>- Witches who practice the clan's own version of blood magic, usually developing an affinity for it in place of Vicissitude.</li> <li><i>The Dracul</i> - A group who eschew affinity for Vicissitude in favor of Dominate, and often keep much closer ties to mortals than others of their clan.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-27T18:28:47-07:00","id":1493878,"title":"","content":"<br /> <b><u>Bloodlines</u></b> (those &quot;lesser&quot; lineages separate from the 13 Clans; they fall into three broad groups)<br /> <br /> <b>The Fallen</b> - The remains of those clans usurped by the Tremere and the Giovanni, having withered over the centuries since their down fall, with members scattered around the globe.<ul><li><b>Salubri</b> - One of the High Clans until the end of the Dark Ages and the uprisings. Once of group of mystics, healers, seers, and exorcists, they had an affinity for Auspex, Fortitude, and their own power of Valeren, and only able to feed on those who don't resist. Survivors include various closeted clan impersonators hidden among the Camarilla, isolated cult leaders, Middle Eastern Sufi mystics, Asian demon hunters, and African soul eaters.</li> <li><b>Cappadocian</b> - Another of the High Clans, destroyed from within during the Renaissance. Devotees to understanding the mysteries of death in all its forms, they had an affinity for Auspex, Fortitude, and Mortis, and a deathly pallor that was difficult to disguise. Since the fall and purge by the Giovanni, surviving groups have grown isolated from each other and sometimes diverge quite a bit. <i>The Sons of Samedi</i>, found mainly in the Caribbean, Brazil, and parts of North America, are primarily voodoo bokkur and mercenaries. Other groups exist in Africa, the Middle East, and India.</li> </ul>[In a perfect world, a sourcebook about the Fallen would've had a full chapter on each of the above, and then an follow up section discussing creating your own Fallen clan as a bloodline, with the True Brujah - with a better name - as the created example.]<br /> <br /> <b>The Created</b> - Groups created artificially by other vampires, usually through forbidden blood magics and other questionable (and sometimes unreliable) methods. There are two main ones.<ul><li><b>The Gargoyles</b> - Created by Tremere experiments to create the perfect warrior-servants, they rebelled during the uprisings at the end of the Dark Ages. For the most part, they look human, save when their Blood Potency reaches higher levels and they aren't using their powers. They have an affinity for Potence, Fortitude, and their own version of Protean which allows them to merge with stone, grow wings, and turn to stone. Their skin is cold and hard to humans, and they have difficulty resisting mental domination.</li> <li><b>The Kiasyd </b>- The product of bizarre experimentations by a Lasombra during the late Dark Ages, involving excursions into Abyss mysticism, the Unseelie Courts, and the Underworld. The end result was something that could best be described as alien and arcane. They universally tend to be tall (but not excessively so) and thin, with skin that is pale even by Kindred standards. They also skew heavily toward intellectual pursuits, some of which make little sense to outsiders. Most prefer their privacy, the better to carry our their studies or experimentations, but small groups will occasionally gather for esoteric purposes. They have an affinity for the disciplines of Auspex, Obfuscate, and Obtenebration, with they are able to combine into alloyed devotions that mimic most of the Mytherceria powers. They take aggravated damage from Iron.</li> </ul><b>The Mysteries</b> - Bloodlines whose origins are completely unknown, and whose existence only raises questions most Kindred probably don't want to know the answers to. The two most prominent, at least in North America are as follows.<ul><li><b>The Daughters of Cacophony </b>- An all female lineage who've only appeared in the past 150 or so years. They tend to bring chaos and upheaval in their wake, and many believe they are a harbinger of the Final Nights. They have an affinity for the disciplines of Fortitude, Presence, and their own unique power of Melpominee, which requires vocal-oriented Performance to use (not limited to singing). Each member is haunted by music only she can hear, which often inevitably drives her to destructive frenzy.</li> <li><b>The </b><b>Tlacique</b> - Originating in Mesoamerica and claiming lineage from none of the 13 Clans. They hold that they are dependents of the blood gods of ancient Mexico and Central America. Their existence points to the strong possibility of Kindred unique to the Americas, totally separate from the 13 known survivors of the Deluge. They have an affinity for Potence, Presence, and Protean (they usually turn into jaguars and vultures rather than wolves and bats). They require extra blood to rise each night.</li> </ul>Anyway, those were the ones we settled on a decade or so ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T12:17:13-07:00","id":1493921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493846#post1493846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Steven Brown and Andrew Greenberg were, as a pair, really bad about coming up with names that were just completely wrong. Hense the ghoul families bearing the name of vengeful spirits risen from the grave</div> </div> </div> </div>Sort of like how ghouls in <i>Vampire</i> have nothing to do with the desert-dwelling, shapeshifting demons of Arabic folklore, isn't it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm","name":"Elphilm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"763"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T12:36:15-07:00","id":1493925,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elphilm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493921#post1493921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sort of like how ghouls in <i>Vampire</i> have nothing to do with the desert-dwelling, shapeshifting demons of Arabic folklore, isn't it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I've yet to hear an explanation for how that came about. Even Lovecraft's ghouls were derived from the original myths. I personally prefer the term Thralls, with Renfield as a more modern slang term among neonates.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T12:47:17-07:00","id":1493926,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure, but I often hear the servants of evil called ghouls and goblins. For example, politicians who serve corporations seem to get called ghouls and goblins by political types who are unlikely to have ever played VTM. I think ghoul must have become slang for the servants of evil, especially political tyrannical evil, a long time ago, and VTM adopted this by calling thralls &quot;ghouls&quot;.<br /> <br /> But yeah, thrall or renfield is a better term.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T17:48:59-07:00","id":1493941,"title":"","content":"I confess I was being tongue-in-cheek above. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> To me, both ghoul and revenant make perfect sense as Kindred slang terms (and that's what they are -- in-character slang, just like all the other terms in the lexicon sections common to all White Wolf game lines). The terms ghoul and revenant even use the same naming logic in the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> Why are vampire thralls called ghouls? Because folkloric ghouls are eaters of the dead. What must mortals do to become vampire thralls? They eat the blood of the dead. It's perfectly sensible that Kindred would come up with a term for their thralls that emphasizes the role of <i>vitae,</i> the only thing they truly care about.<br /> <br /> Why are hereditary ghouls called revenants? Because the word doesn't <i>just</i> mean &quot;vengeful spirits risen from the grave.&quot; That is one of the <i>connotations</i> of the term, but <i>revenant</i> is simply the present participle of the French <i>revenir,</i> and can mean any person or thing returning (and not just from beyond the grave). What is the one thing that is unique to hereditary ghouls in the World of Darkness? They are the only creatures, living or dead, who produce vampiric <i>vitae</i> naturally. It's the blood that keeps returning, and again the slang term emphasizes this fact.<br /> <br /> I don't think Steven C. Brown and Andrew Greenberg were nearly as bad as was suggested above when it came to choosing names and terms for the material they developed. On the contrary, I find that often their additions to early <i>Vampire</i> are more in line with the logic and thinking behind Rein-Hagen's original naming conventions than the work of later authors is. For example, I have <i>thoughts</i> about the Nagaraja that are not kind to the V20 era writers, but maybe that's a topic for another time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm","name":"Elphilm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"763"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T22:48:46-07:00","id":1493951,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493870#post1493870\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The Witches of Echidna were created by the authors to highlight the Typhon/Set conflict within the main clan and, indeed, within Set himself</div> </div> </div> </div>So just use Settites. There's no merit to bringing in <i>True Settites</i> with radical beliefs we've never heard of before. <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (Echidna being Typhon's wife in Greek mythology). That's also why they are Greek rather than calling themselves Witches of Nephthys or anything else. The idea of Typhon and Greek mythology is pretty important within the concept of VtM Set. </div> </div> </div> And you're missing the irony that before the egyptian Settites got onto the greek path of typhon they had to eliminate the greek witches of echidna?<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They were also intended to help set the stage for what we see in V5 with the Setite civil war, which is one of the V5 developments that I think is pretty cool. </div> </div> </div><b> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Though yeah, if you don't like any of those things then it's natural to dislike the bloodline. </div> </div> </div> </b>One of the recuring themes with 'setting the stage for V5 developments' is that you need do stupid things to justify other stupid things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also note that while they're called &quot;Witches&quot; the books make it clear that there are many male members as well as female members, so I don't know that they're supposed to be Girl Power or anything. </div> </div> </div> Well there's the whole &quot;mother of monsters&quot; and &quot;damn those jealous chauvinist priests&quot; shtick they've got going on.<br /> <br /> <br /> Really, the latter half of V20's run fell apart. DAV20 was a mess and Lore of The Bloodlines would have put me off most bloodlines were there not much better works before them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 09-29-2022, 01:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-29T00:23:07-07:00","id":1493962,"title":"","content":"I've generally liked the Setites to a degree but my biggest problem is that the they come off as this kumbaya group. But they're a crazy Cult of supernatural demi-gods. The history of the Followers of Set should be one of bloody religious battles, orthodox Setites, reformed Setites, revivalsists ultra-orthodox, etc. All of them utterly convinced of the correctness of their beliefs and, often, willing to murder their rivals within the clan.<br /> <br /> We never really got that in the older editions. There's a lot of stuff I don't like about V5 but felt like it was about time to see a religious schism among the Setites. And I think even without a V5 the metaplot was moving towards a religious schism anyway. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I don't recall seeing much on the way of Anti-male stuff in their discussion in Tome of Sectets.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-29T02:13:12-07:00","id":1493965,"title":"","content":"Given the gnostic basis of their beliefs it actually makes a lot of sense that -for the most part- they don't have a lot of schisms. Gods are Bad; reality is a prison; Everyone has a bit of divinity in them; our chosen rebel God has shown us the way to break down the walls of our prison: we can awaken everyone by making reality suck to the point that people reject it by embracing the tenets of anarcho capitalism (Drugs are good, age is just a number!) which is honestly pretty good for vampires anyway. It doesn't matter if you call him Set or Caine, so long as there are enough illicit pleasures to go around. While I'm sure there are many individual disagreements among the Settites it's pretty unsurprising that nobody wants to ruin a good thing (especially after having gone through an indoctrination process that excludes anyone likely to cause problems. While one or two might fall through the cracks, it's not going to be enough for a proper schism)<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DAV20 p450</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Witches serve the mother. No matter the form, no matter the name, the costume, or the dogma, the Witches serve the mother. Echidna ripples through their hearts, growing through her cycles to meet the new age. Though the priests of Set confuse the semantic in their fanatic Hellenistic fervor, they have ever done their part to push the clan forward \u2013 to keep it young and to keep the mother\u2019s coils wrapping round the world. And for this, the Witches are grateful. While the Warriors of Glycon vie for dominion and control and the Priests of the aardvark-headed god spread her sermon, the Witches of Echidna serve</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Yes, the priests are deluded as they worship a man because we're all worshipping the mother of all evils...&quot; Yeah I'm getting strong Bahari vibes here. The insinuation that they were once all equal in a trinity but the witches were killed because the men couldn't handle the truth is pretty grating for someone that likes the FoS. You know I don't actually hate the Bahari, but they've got their own place, the writers don't need to undermine every strong man with a woman who knows the truth, much less use them in a way that suggests it's the actual truth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-29T12:52:01-07:00","id":1493990,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493962#post1493962\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've generally liked the Setites to a degree but my biggest problem is that the they come off as this kumbaya group. But they're a crazy Cult of supernatural demi-gods. The history of the Followers of Set should be one of bloody religious battles, orthodox Setites, reformed Setites, revivalsists ultra-orthodox, etc. All of them utterly convinced of the correctness of their beliefs and, often, willing to murder their rivals within the clan.<br /> <br /> We never really got that in the older editions.</div> </div> </div> </div>We do see aspects of the now and then. The Serpents of the Light are an example of such a group of heretics. The reason they entered the Sabbat was so they could be protected against the Setite reprisal. If it happened once, it could have happened again but the heretic crushed before it could lead to anything big. And the first edition of World of Darkness describes the Setites in Haiti as particular divided and involved in internecine warfare because they lack a dominant leader who imposes her will on the other temples. And in the original clan description we're told that Set himself eventually changed the entire culture of the clan after one defeat and recreated the clan again from scratch (implying that he possibly destroyed any older Setites who had also survived).<br /> <br /> But we don't see this described in the clanbooks. This is one area where we must be careful about the IC nature of much of the writing found in the clanbooks.<br /> <br /> I don't think the overall Setite leadership allows such factions to become big enough to threaten the unity of the entire clan. They crush them and destroy any heretics as early as possible. So no big revolts or civil wars, but that is only because the mow the grass on a frequent basis to prevent big weeds from growing. <br /> <br /> There is probably a fair amount of political intrigue between leaders of various temples provided they are local to each other. A chaotic situation like Haiti should happen any time the population of local Setites gets high enough that there are multiple nearby temples, but without an obvious, dominant leader imposing his will on the others.<br /> <br /> That is how I would run it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493965#post1493965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given the gnostic basis of their beliefs it actually makes a lot of sense that -for the most part- they don't have a lot of schisms.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given the sheer number of different Gnostic religions, I wouldn't agree. There seems to be lots of schisms. What you don't have is one group of Gnostics using force to suppress or convert another group, but that is because the Gnostics never gained control of a polity and could thereby use force against the others. And there could have easily been thuggish violence between different factions that simply was not recorded in the documents we have.<br /> <br /> But within the Setite clan, you had the explicit dictatorial leadership of Set himself for thousands of years. And then whatever leader or leaders who followed him (likely hand picked). That would give the dominant faction able to enforce official doctrines or practice out of legitimacy and personal power.<br /> <br /> Personally, I like the earlier concept of Setites as priests of the Wyrm. The Revised era depiction of them somehow being Gnostics was done specifically to take the Setites away from their roots in Robert E. Howard. But I understand there are many who like the Setites to be a wider concept.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-29T13:28:46-07:00","id":1493994,"title":"","content":"Probably the biggest aid to Settite unity is the part where they're vampires. I know, normally the blood suckers have more reasons to be in conflict with one another, not less. But the vampire condition really throws in a few curveballs<br /> -There's not that many of them<br /> -They mostly stay in their own domains and administering a cult/supplying drugs/children/&quot;Don't tread on me&quot; merchandise is a lot of busywork. <br /> -They're a minority religion having to work with what's usually a camarilla majority. <br /> -Survival and self-interest is on the mind as much as theology. <br /> <br /> There's really more to lose for everyone involved to really get into theological spats lightly. Unless someone's doing something that'll drag the settites down massively like Infernalism there's little reason to get too involved. Plus everyone's assuming their sire lied to them a bit so knowing the one-true-way is not really a luxury settites have.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-29T18:48:54-07:00","id":1494007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493965#post1493965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given the gnostic basis of their beliefs it actually makes a lot of sense that -for the most part- they don't have a lot of schisms. Gods are Bad; reality is a prison; Everyone has a bit of divinity in them; our chosen rebel God has shown us the way to break down the walls of our prison: we can awaken everyone by making reality suck to the point that people reject it by embracing the tenets of anarcho capitalism (Drugs are good, age is just a number!) which is honestly pretty good for vampires anyway. It doesn't matter if you call him Set or Caine, so long as there are enough illicit pleasures to go around. While I'm sure there are many individual disagreements among the Settites it's pretty unsurprising that nobody wants to ruin a good thing (especially after having gone through an indoctrination process that excludes anyone likely to cause problems. While one or two might fall through the cracks, it's not going to be enough for a proper schism)</div> </div> </div> </div>Simply from a gaming perspective I find that more boring than having the Setites be a viper's den of warring sects. The clan is formed from a bunch of religious fanatics who are not only separated by thousands of miles but also by thousands of years. It's only natural that there would be drift in their beliefs and each fanatic would be convinced that his or her beliefs are more correct than anyone else's and this would turn into conflict between clan members. That clan conflict would occasionally spill over outside of the clan.<br /> <br /> We pretty much never see that and the one time we did, with the Serpents of Light, it was held up as exceptional. I've always thought it was a lost opportunity to make the clan more interesting, play more roles and have more options at the gaming table. The Ministry/Followers split we see in 5th edition isn't exactly the direction I would have taken things, but it feels like it's better than the status quo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T01:27:10-07:00","id":1494026,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure the clan would have its spats. Set is Dead, let's ressurect him VS Set is inside of all of us VS set is this kid right now VS Set is hiding somewhere. I'm pretty sure Settites would have elders and broods that despise eachother. But a full blown schism? The theology is pretty well designed to resist that. (I believe the Indian Settites also don't like the egyptians but it's not serpents-of-the-light levels of hostile. You also have the CoO if you want to consider that a Set schism)<br /> <br /> <br /> Also you've got all the cults that aren't obviously Set related: Gehenna cults, infernalists, Bahari, Assamites, Ravnos, anarch new religions... You don't really need religious schisms to have religious enemies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T02:04:11-07:00","id":1494029,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1493846#post1493846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> Steven Brown and Andrew Greenberg were, as a pair, really bad about coming up with names that were just completely wrong. Hense the ghoul families bearing the name of vengeful spirits risen from the grave, a 19th century Appalachian offshoot of the Gangrel bearing the name of the ancient Persian demon-god of darkness and lies,</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, calling them cougars, pumas or bobcats might have given people the wrong kind of idea....<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> But yeah, a name somehow resonant of appalachian or at least american folklore, native or not, as a whole would have been a much better fit.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 09-30-2022, 05:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T02:19:31-07:00","id":1494030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494029#post1494029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, calling them cougars, pumas or bobcats might have given people the wrong kind of idea....<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> But yeah, a name somehow resonant of appalachian or at least american folklore, native or not, as a whole would have been a much better fit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wampus, maybe? I'm not sure anyone would have taken that seriously, though, even if you nicknamed them Hellcats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T02:24:14-07:00","id":1494031,"title":"","content":"As far as the Setites, I've occasionally fiddled with the idea of having the Dark Ages European ones be known as Sethites and entirely associated with ophian and antinomian flavors of Sethian gnosticism.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T05:37:09-07:00","id":1494035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494031#post1494031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as the Setites, I've occasionally fiddled with the idea of having the Dark Ages European ones be known as Sethites and entirely associated with ophian and antinomian flavors of Sethian gnosticism.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> In my games long ago i mostly just remade them as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shesha\" target=\"_blank\">Followers of Shesha</a>, what also gives me a pretty good cultural foothold not only in the Indian subcontinent but Southeast Asia to boot, extra entertaining when one considers the Khmer culture mythological association with Shesha's naga children.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T06:13:21-07:00","id":1494036,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494026#post1494026\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm pretty sure the clan would have its spats. Set is Dead, let's ressurect him VS Set is inside of all of us VS set is this kid right now VS Set is hiding somewhere. I'm pretty sure Settites would have elders and broods that despise eachother.</div> </div> </div> </div>You would think so but we don't really see it. I just think the Followers would be more interesting with more varied ideas and factions within the clan than what we traditionally had. Which, again, is why I kind of liked the Witches for highlighting that and also V5 for introducing it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T11:49:25-07:00","id":1494048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494029#post1494029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But yeah, a name somehow resonant of appalachian or at least american folklore, native or not, as a whole would have been a much better fit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the connection to Appalachia is incidental, not important, to the identity of the Ahrimanes. We know that they were originally Gangrel. We know Muricia established herself with the local Indian population and studied their WoD magical practices in an attempt to break her Vinculum with her sire (who is implied to be one of the two &quot;founding&quot; Gangrel elders of the anti-tribu). That created the ritual that could turn certain vampires into Ahrimanes, and allowed the development of Spiritus. They are known for being warriors and survivors. They are secretive and violent. They are also known for their material wealth. Muricia believes men are inferior to women and prone to conflict, and limits the Ahrimanes just to women. They usually only form packs among their own bloodline. They have limited contact with their own sect, but do provide aid to the Sabbat by keeping them informed of Lupine activity in the wilderness. Ahrimanes do not wander, but stay in their own communities and remain heavily involved in mortal communities and use mortal pawns (which is not a very Sabbat thing to do, as that usually gets passed on to the Revenants).<br /> <br /> Muricia and the original Ahrimanes seem to have been from Spanish colonial stock originally, which implies Muricia and her followers were likely in the southern Appalachia in modern Georgia, Alabama, and Mississippi which was the area where the Spanish had the most contact. (And I just looked up the original bloodline description, it is this tier of southern states - plus Louisiana - that is mentioned, not Appalachia) If I had to pick one people that provided the &quot;native shamans&quot;, I would pick the Muscogee. But I don't think there are too many cultural ties to the native Indians who happened to live here. What was important to Muricia was the WoD magical knowledge certain people there had, not necessarily any cultural connection.<br /> <br /> This group sounds a lot like a cult that is building its own army and economic network to survive an upcoming apocalypse (probably Gehenna). Even Muricia's selection of only female seems less due to some kind of modern feminism (which would be anachronistic to the time she likely transformed - somewhere between 1500-1700), but more like she thought the two male Gangrel Sabbat elders were foolishly feuding and it was consuming the Gangrel anti-tribu and making it weaker. She just attributes this infighting as a natural flaw in males, and believes females are more group oriented and cooperative.<br /> <br /> If we assume that the name Ahrimanes for this bloodline is supposed to make sense somehow, the best interpretation is that the name was taken because of Ahriman as an adversary figures. It's just that in the bloodline's case, it isn't against Ahura Mazda, but probably the antedeluvians. That may not have been the actual case, and the name was picked randomly as many suspect.<br /> <br /> So if I renamed the bloodline, I'd probably wouldn't pick some random association with Appalachia. But try to tie it into something that can be associated with an apocalyptic doomsday cult of prepper survivalists... that happen to be exclusively women.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T15:28:13-07:00","id":1494059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494048#post1494048\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the connection to Appalachia is incidental, not important, to the identity of the Ahrimanes. We know that they were originally Gangrel. We know Muricia established herself with the local Indian population and studied their WoD magical practices in an attempt to break her Vinculum with her sire (who is implied to be one of the two &quot;founding&quot; Gangrel elders of the anti-tribu). That created the ritual that could turn certain vampires into Ahrimanes, and allowed the development of Spiritus. They are known for being warriors and survivors. They are secretive and violent. They are also known for their material wealth. Muricia believes men are inferior to women and prone to conflict, and limits the Ahrimanes just to women. They usually only form packs among their own bloodline. They have limited contact with their own sect, but do provide aid to the Sabbat by keeping them informed of Lupine activity in the wilderness. Ahrimanes do not wander, but stay in their own communities and remain heavily involved in mortal communities and use mortal pawns (which is not a very Sabbat thing to do, as that usually gets passed on to the Revenants).<br /> <br /> Muricia and the original Ahrimanes seem to have been from Spanish colonial stock originally, which implies Muricia and her followers were likely in the southern Appalachia in modern Georgia, Alabama, and Mississippi which was the area where the Spanish had the most contact. (And I just looked up the original bloodline description, it is this tier of southern states - plus Louisiana - that is mentioned, not Appalachia) If I had to pick one people that provided the &quot;native shamans&quot;, I would pick the Muscogee. But I don't think there are too many cultural ties to the native Indians who happened to live here. What was important to Muricia was the WoD magical knowledge certain people there had, not necessarily any cultural connection.<br /> <br /> This group sounds a lot like a cult that is building its own army and economic network to survive an upcoming apocalypse (probably Gehenna). Even Muricia's selection of only female seems less due to some kind of modern feminism (which would be anachronistic to the time she likely transformed - somewhere between 1500-1700), but more like she thought the two male Gangrel Sabbat elders were foolishly feuding and it was consuming the Gangrel anti-tribu and making it weaker. She just attributes this infighting as a natural flaw in males, and believes females are more group oriented and cooperative.<br /> <br /> If we assume that the name Ahrimanes for this bloodline is supposed to make sense somehow, the best interpretation is that the name was taken because of Ahriman as an adversary figures. It's just that in the bloodline's case, it isn't against Ahura Mazda, but probably the antedeluvians. That may not have been the actual case, and the name was picked randomly as many suspect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, not that it means that much, but i'd like to point out i didn't limit myself to just Appalachian folklore when i made that comment about renaming them to something more &quot;culturally resonant&quot;. But yeah, it has been decades since i last gave the Ahrimanes a serious read.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494048#post1494048\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So if I renamed the bloodline, I'd probably wouldn't pick some random association with Appalachia. But try to tie it into something that can be associated with an apocalyptic doomsday cult of prepper survivalists... that happen to be exclusively women.</div> </div> </div> </div>Something that somehow tied into any local shamanistic traditions or terms might also fit with the subject of Muricia's personal/spiritual quest, i guess.<br /> What <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Yaryan\" target=\"_blank\">reminds</a> me... (Incidentally, his discipline set makes this NPC closer to the two Gangrel branches than the Brujah he's supposed to be).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 09-30-2022, 06:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T17:04:55-07:00","id":1494064,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493721-bloodlines-the-best-and-the-worst?p=1494030#post1494030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wampus, maybe? I'm not sure anyone would have taken that seriously, though, even if you nicknamed them Hellcats.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Cherokee have something called a Death Cat that could work pretty well.<br /> <br /> Edit: Apparently that is the same thing, maybe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Exalted Whose Name is Carved in Leaves of Jade<br /> Senator of the 